CAT = "nmlsApplication"; SEG = "APP"
Q = [
("Which form is the standard residential mortgage application used by Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac?",
 "Uniform Residential Loan Application", ["Uniform Settlement Statement", "Uniform Underwriting Transmittal", "Uniform Residential Appraisal Report"],
 "The Uniform Residential Loan Application (Form 1003/65) collects borrower, property, employment, asset and liability information. The appraisal report is a separate form."),
("A borrower applies for an adjustable-rate mortgage. Under Regulation Z, the lender must give the CHARM booklet:",
 "At application or before any fee", ["At closing with the loan note", "Within 30 days after closing", "Only if the rate first adjusts"],
 "The Consumer Handbook on Adjustable-Rate Mortgages and the loan program disclosure are due at application or before the consumer pays a nonrefundable fee, whichever is earlier."),
("For a purchase loan application, the lender must give the borrower the CFPB's Your Home Loan Toolkit within:",
 "Three business days of applying", ["Seven business days of applying", "Ten days before closing", "One business day after approval"],
 "RESPA requires the special information booklet, now the Home Loan Toolkit, within three business days after a purchase-money application."),
("A borrower changed jobs four months ago in the same line of work after six years with the old employer. When reviewing employment, the lender generally looks for:",
 "A two-year history in the field", ["Five years at the new employer", "No change in the last two years", "One year at the new employer"],
 "Underwriters generally verify a two-year employment and income history. A recent move within the same field with steady or higher pay is usually acceptable."),
("A self-employed borrower applies for a conventional loan. To document income, the lender will most often require:",
 "Two years of tax returns", ["Three months of pay stubs", "One year of bank statements only", "A letter from the borrower's CPA only"],
 "Self-employment income is usually averaged from the last two years of personal and business federal tax returns, with a profit and loss statement where needed."),
("A buyer's parents give $25,000 toward the down payment. The lender will usually require:",
 "A signed gift letter", ["A promissory note from the parents", "A second lien in the parents' name", "A copy of the parents' tax return"],
 "A gift letter states the amount, the donor's relationship and that no repayment is expected, and the lender documents the transfer of funds. A loan from the parents would be a debt, not a gift."),
("A borrower's bank statement shows a large deposit that is not from payroll. The originator should:",
 "Document the source of the deposit", ["Ignore it since the funds are there", "Remove that statement from the file", "Ask the borrower to move it elsewhere"],
 "Large or unusual deposits must be sourced and explained in writing, since undisclosed loans or illegal funds may be behind them."),
("A borrower earns $9,000 a month gross. The proposed PITI is $2,070 and other monthly debts are $900. The back-end debt-to-income ratio is:",
 "33%", ["23%", "10%", "39%"],
 "Total monthly obligations divided by gross income: ($2,070 + $900) / $9,000 = 33%. The housing (front-end) ratio alone is 23%."),
("A borrower earns $6,500 a month. Using a 31% housing ratio, the highest monthly housing payment allowed is:",
 "$2,015", ["$2,795", "$1,950", "$2,100"],
 "The housing payment limit is gross monthly income times the front-end ratio: $6,500 x 0.31 = $2,015."),
("A borrower has three credit scores: 702, 676 and 731. For a single borrower, most lenders qualify using:",
 "702", ["676", "731", "703, the average"],
 "With three bureau scores, lenders usually use the middle one, here 702. With two scores, the lower is used."),
("To verify a borrower's income directly with the IRS, the lender asks the borrower to sign:",
 "Form 4506-C", ["Form W-9", "Form 1099-INT", "Form 1003"],
 "IRS Form 4506-C authorizes the lender to get tax transcripts through the IRS income verification program to check the returns the borrower provided."),
("Which appraisal approach is given the most weight for a typical owner-occupied single-family home?",
 "Sales comparison approach", ["Cost approach", "Income approach", "Gross rent multiplier only"],
 "The sales comparison (market) approach, adjusting recent comparable sales, is the main approach for one-unit homes. The cost approach suits new or unique buildings; the income approach suits rentals."),
("A loan officer calls the appraiser and says the deal needs a value of at least $410,000. Under Regulation Z's valuation independence rules, this is:",
 "Prohibited", ["Allowed if the value is accurate", "Allowed if the appraiser agrees", "Allowed if the buyer asks"],
 "Regulation Z bars anyone with an interest in the transaction from coercing, instructing or inducing an appraiser to reach a target value. Asking the appraiser to fix factual errors is allowed."),
("A title commitment issued before closing shows:",
 "Exceptions to title coverage", ["The final closing costs", "The appraised value of the home", "The borrower's credit history"],
 "The commitment lists the vested owner, liens and other exceptions, and the requirements that must be met before the title insurer issues the policy."),
("For a purchase loan, the loan-to-value ratio is based on:",
 "The lower of price or appraised value", ["The higher of price or appraised value", "The appraised value in all cases", "The tax-assessed value of the home"],
 "Lenders base LTV on the lower of the sales price or appraised value, so a high price does not inflate the loan amount."),
("A buyer offers $420,000 for a home that appraises at $400,000 and asks for a 90% LTV loan. The maximum loan amount is:",
 "$360,000", ["$378,000", "$400,000", "$342,000"],
 "LTV uses the lower of price and value: 0.90 x $400,000 = $360,000. Using the contract price would give $378,000."),
("A borrower receives the Loan Estimate and does not indicate intent to proceed. The settlement charges are no longer binding after:",
 "10 business days", ["3 business days", "30 calendar days", "60 calendar days"],
 "If the consumer does not express intent to proceed within 10 business days after the Loan Estimate is provided, the creditor may issue a revised estimate with new charges."),
("After the Loan Estimate is issued, the appraisal comes in low and the borrower must take a different loan amount. The creditor may issue a revised Loan Estimate within:",
 "Three business days of learning of it", ["Seven business days of learning of it", "Ten business days of learning of it", "One business day before closing"],
 "A valid changed circumstance lets the creditor reset tolerances by providing a revised Loan Estimate within three business days of receiving the information."),
("Which document must a lender provide at application to disclose whether it intends to service the loan or transfer it?",
 "Servicing disclosure statement", ["Affiliated business disclosure", "Initial escrow statement", "Notice of right to rescind"],
 "Regulation X requires a servicing disclosure for first-lien loans within three business days of application, stating whether the servicing may be assigned, sold or transferred."),
("An application lacks a pay stub the lender needs to make a decision. Under Regulation B, the creditor may:",
 "Send a notice of incompleteness", ["Deny it without any notice", "Hold it open for 12 months", "Withdraw it on the borrower's behalf"],
 "When an application is incomplete, the creditor may send a written notice listing the missing information and a reasonable deadline, or treat it as an adverse action notice."),
("A borrower has rented out a property for years and the lease shows $2,000 a month. Under common agency guidelines, the lender typically counts as gross rental income:",
 "$1,500 a month", ["$2,000 a month", "$1,000 a month", "$1,800 a month"],
 "Fannie Mae and FHA usually count 75% of gross rent from a lease to allow for vacancy and maintenance: 0.75 x $2,000 = $1,500."),
("A borrower earned $8,400 of overtime last year and $6,000 the year before, and expects it to continue. Averaged over two years, the monthly overtime income is:",
 "$600", ["$700", "$500", "$1,200"],
 "Variable income is averaged over 24 months when stable: ($8,400 + $6,000) / 24 = $600. Using only last year gives $700."),
("A borrower's 18-year-old daughter, who will not live in the home, co-signs to help qualify. Lenders call her a:",
 "Non-occupant co-borrower", ["Power of attorney holder", "Occupant co-borrower", "Third-party originator"],
 "A non-occupant co-borrower is obligated on the note and adds income but does not live in the property. Many programs limit or set rules for them."),
("A borrower who is deployed overseas wants a relative to sign closing documents. The lender should:",
 "Review a valid power of attorney", ["Let the relative sign without papers", "Postpone the loan until return", "Have the loan officer sign instead"],
 "A specific, valid power of attorney meeting the lender's and investor's rules can let an agent sign. The originator may never sign for a borrower."),
("Which hazard insurance requirement is standard on a purchase mortgage?",
 "Coverage in force by closing", ["Coverage within 60 days after closing", "Coverage only for loans over 80% LTV", "Coverage paid by the lender"],
 "The lender requires an insurance policy naming it as mortgagee, effective at or before closing, to protect the collateral."),
("An applicant answers on the URLA that the home will be a primary residence. This matters because occupancy affects:",
 "Pricing and program eligibility", ["Only the appraisal fee", "Only the title insurance premium", "Only the credit report fee"],
 "Owner-occupied loans usually get better pricing and access to FHA, VA and low down payment programs, which is why occupancy fraud is common."),
("The CFPB's ability-to-repay rule requires the lender to verify income using:",
 "Reliable third-party records", ["The borrower's statement alone", "The real estate agent's estimate", "A recent credit report alone"],
 "Income and assets must be verified with reliable third-party records such as W-2s, tax returns, payroll records or bank statements."),
("A lender must give an applicant written notice of the right to receive a copy of the appraisal within:",
 "Three business days of applying", ["Ten business days of applying", "Three days before closing", "Thirty days after closing"],
 "Regulation B requires notice of the right to a copy of each appraisal or written valuation within three business days after receiving an application for a first-lien dwelling loan."),
]
